steve barclay is the prime minister's  new chief of staff  he's already a minister and will now be  responsible for implementing the new  office of the prime minister  well it's all supposed to be a part of a  big clear out and reorganization at  number 10 in the wake of the partygate  scandal as a prime minister fights for  survival  so just how bad are things at number 10  and how will those that are left be  feeling  gavin barwell was theresa may's chief of  staff during some of the most turbulent  brexit years he joins me now  good morning lord barwell  now  given them  given that the prime minister was the  chief tormentor of your administration  can you tell me with a straight face  that you take no pleasure from his  present difficulties  yeah i can because i don't take any  pleasure of the difficulties that my  party is in at the moment and from the  point of view of the country uh you know  i don't think it's  anything anyone can take uh any pleasure  in at all trevor uh no it's not not an  out to shard and freud no temptation to  say i told you so because you did  right well so so there's a look i think  i'm we're perfectly entitled to warn uh  to say to people that the concerns that  we raised both about the brexit approach  the prime minister was going to take  you've just had an interview with  jeffrey donaldson there  uh teresa absolutely warned what a  border down the rsc might mean for our  politics so i think we're perfectly  entitled to take to say i told you so  but that's a different thing from what  you asked me which is do i take any  pleasure uh in seeing that the mess that  things are at the moment and i don't  okay well that's a very grown-up  approach um and of course that's why uh  the prime minister before previous prime  minister had you in there you were her  chief of staff um  steve barkley is your successor is that  a good appointment  so i've got a lot of time for steve i  think he's a really smart person uh  incredibly diligent uh he doesn't take  any nonsense he'll want high standards  there  i should declare an interest he's  someone i would consider a friend so  lots of good qualities that he has i  think that there are two challenges  the first is  he won't be able to do the job in the  way that i or nick timothy and fiona  hill or ed luellen or jonathan powell  did it because he's combining it with  being a government minister and an mp at  the same time so he's going to have to  find a way of doing the job in a  different way  and then the second challenge and i  think probably the key one  is whether the prime minister is going  to listen to the good advice that he  will give  you know a lot of the focus over the  last week or two has been about making  changes as downing street as if that  will  solve the problem it's worth  remembering that dan rosenfeld has just  announced he's standing down as chief of  staff  he was brought in by the prime minister  to clear up the previous mess in number  10. so there's only so much different  advisers can make  if the person at the top is not actually  listening to the advice that we're given  and to me  the most interesting thing about the  events of the last week or so  was minera murz's letter where it was  made clear that actually the prime  minister was told  by his advisors  not to make that smear about kierstarma  last monday  and ignored that advice and and a  statement that was meant to  show contrition and draw a line under  this story actually ended up making his  position so much worse  um just on on that point  um the business secretary just  a few minutes ago said to me that it  seemed a perfectly reasonable thing to  say in context  well i completely disagree with that i  mean if i if i imagine a high political  world where i had been advising boris  johnson before he went into the chamber  on monday  i would have said under no circumstances  either make any criticisms of your  opponents or make any excuses the job  today  is to go into the chamber and show  genuine contrition because that is your  only hope of drawing a line uh under  this story so i both think on an  intellectual level and a moral level  what he said was not acceptable i don't  want a politics there's enough there's  enough things trevor you'll know from  the people you interview there's enough  legitimate differences between us for  those to be the subject of political  debate and argument we don't want a  politics where people indulge in those  kind of smears but even if you set aside  that moral argument on a purely tactical  level it was a stupid thing for the  prime minister to do because it's led to  the resignation of one of his key aides  it's led to more letters more mps  submitting letters of no confidence in  him it's further  destabilized his position and the point  here is that he we know  he was given advice not to do that and  went ahead and did it anyway so what  that tells you is that yes having the  right people around you can help but  ultimately the culture in number 10 and  the decisions the government makes come  from the top and the prime minister  himself  if he's going to stay in office is going  to have to change the way he is behaving  and running the government  isn't the problem here then and i speak  to you as an experienced advisor  that actually  he doesn't take advice and you could  have socrates machiavelli  and whoever else you liked in there  to advice him  but he when he's got it in his head  won't take that advice cannot help  himself and he will do what he wants  isn't the problem here it's not the  advisors not the circle around him it's  the prime minister  so that that definitely has been the  problem and the question is whether it's  going to change the other person that  he's  bringing back into number uh bringing in  to number 10 is the new director of  communications good harry who was one of  his key advisors when he was mayor of  london so there was a period as you all  know from your own experience  when he was running london where first  with simon milton then with eddie lister  he had a team around him that he did  seem to to listen to and respect so it  is possible  uh that these changes you know he might  pay more attention to this new team than  he was to the previous one but you're  absolutely right about what the problem  has been up until now  uh it  i don't want to take too much time on  this specific point but  every time  one of these things happens you know  he says there was no party oh yes there  was a party uh i was another party oh  yes i was at the party uh  there's whatever it is about kirsten  that somebody says  uh he's going to change he says it's  going to change and then he does it all  over again  yes i think  at some point in the future when  someone's writing a book about how to  handle political scandals the events of  the last few months will be like chapter  one of how not to do it  now if you imagine a hypothetical world  where when this story was first raised  number 10 had quickly established the  facts put them out there issued a  genuinely contrite apology  that might have been okay to get through  that what is indisputable  is that the way that the truth has been  dragged out of them  over the last two or three months has  made this situation far far worse far  more damaging to the prime minister's  reputation we're at a point today  where this where somewhere between 60  and 70  of your viewers think the prime minister  should resign  and ultimately  you know whatever comes out of the  police investigation or the final  version of the sue grey report if he  can't turn those numbers around if his  personal polling remains where it is  today then conservative mps are going to  make a change sooner or later  well well you've been you've been kind  enough to be one of our viewers today um  where are you on that uh which side of  that line are you on should he go as the  60 or 70  and for the country so i am on on on on  the moral issue i find it very very  difficult to defend the way in which he  has run number 10 and the way in which  he has tried to cover up what's happened  and on the pragmatic test which your  viewers will probably have less sympathy  with about what's the right thing for  the conservative party to do  i am very skeptical  about whether it's going to be possible  for him to restore his standing up with  the british public so my inclination is  that the conservative party would be  better making a change  and i also think uh for the good of a  country in terms of trust and faith in  our politics there's a strong case for  change but it's not up to me to make a  decision ultimately this is a decision  uh that up until the next election at  least is one for conservative mps  well and it's a decision for him so if  you were his advisor  would you be saying  boris time's up i don't think there's uh  a chance in hell that the prime minister  is going to voluntarily resign he's  going to stay there uh unless  conservative mps remove him or unless he  loses an election uh and so  you know i don't see any prospect of him  voluntarily standing  downtrend gavin barwell thank you so  much for your time and your  experience this morning  my pleasure

